    "response": "The morning mist clung to the cobblestones of our weary city like a shroud, dampening the usual clangor of the baker's wheel and the distant tolling of St. Jude's bell. I stood at my counter, dusting flour upon an empty slate, when the heavy oak door burst open with a flourish that echoed through the silent shop. There entered Lady Elara Vance, her gown of midnight blue trimmed in silver thread, accompanied by a retinue of servants whose faces wore expressions of profound confusion rather than customary reverence. She did not approach the counter as one might expect; instead, she swept past me toward the back room, where my master, Mr. Thorne, had been seen earlier humming a tune that was both joyous and utterly discordant. As I watched, the air seemed to thicken with unspoken tension. One of the shopkeepers, an old man named Silas who had known my father's face since before he could read, dropped his ledger with a clatter that sounded like a gunshot. He stared at Lady Vance as if she were a ghost materialized from the ether, his knuckles white against his apron. Another young lad, tasked with polishing the brass display cases, halted mid-broom and froze, his eyes wide with a mixture of terror and awe. They whispered amongst themselves in hushed tones, their voices barely rising above the low draft from the chimney, yet carrying an urgency that vibrated through my very bones. Did they fear her presence? Or did they wonder if the invitation to the grand ball mentioned in the morning's broadsheets had indeed reached them? The silence that followed was heavier than the storm clouds gathering overhead, a profound stillness that demanded I look away, yet my gaze could not help but track the strange procession moving toward the rear of our humble establishment.",
